"CONSUMER CONTRACTS\non an uninsured-motorist bodily-injury form); Duhon v. Activelaf, LLC, 192 So. 3d 762 (La. 2016)\n(Louisiana: denying enforcement after finding that there was insufficient notice because consumer\nwas asked to check a box on a screen next to a lengthy arbitration clause that was buried among\nother terms); Caspi v. Microsoft Networks LLC, 732 A.2d 528, 532 (N.J. Super. Ct. App. Div.\n1999) (New Jersey: finding no violation of the public policy of New Jersey in enforcing the\nmembership agreement for an online computer service in which prospective subscribers were\nrequired to click either \"I agree\" or \"I don't agree\" to terms); Barnett v. Network Sols., Inc., 38\nS.W.3d 200 (Tex. App. 2001) (Texas: enforcing the forum-selection clause in an agreement in\nwhich plaintiff had to electronically scroll through the contract in order to accept its provisions);\nHomeAdvisor, Inc. v. Waddell, 2020 WL 2988565 (Tex. App. June 4, 2020) (Texas: holding that\nconspicuous placement of the terms and conditions and the clear, unambiguous notice instructing\nconsumer that clicking the \"submit\" button indicates consent were sufficient to constitute assent);\nMinnik v. Clearwire U.S. LLC, 275 P.3d 1127 (Wash. 2012) (Washington: determining that a fee\nwas an alternative-performance provision and not a liquidated-damages provision in a clickwrap\nagreement); Hastings v. Unikrn, Inc., 12 Wash. App. 2d 1072 (Wash. Ct. App. 2020) (Washington:\nfinding insufficient notice because the link to the terms and conditions could not alert a reasonably\nprudent user that clicking the affirmation box would also constitute assent to the terms); State ex\nrel. U-Haul Co. v. Zakaib, 752 S.E.2d 586 (W. Va. 2013) (West Virginia: recognizing the\nlegitimacy of clickwrap and other electronic contracts, but holding that U-Haul was unsuccessful\nin its attempts to incorporate an addendum because it was only provided to customers after the\nrental agreement was executed); Doe v. Massage Envy Franchising, LLC, 303 Cal. Rptr. 3d 269\n(Cal. Ct. App. 2022); Sellers v. JustAnswer LLC, 289 Cal. Rptr. 3d 1 (Cal. Ct. App. 2021), reh'g\ndenied (Jan. 18, 2022) (California: concluding that the presentation of terms for automatic renewal\nof the service did not provide sufficient notice because the layout, presentation, and font of the\nterms did not call attention to the language); Sarchi v. Uber Techs., Inc., 268 A.3d 258 (Me. 2022)\n(Maine: applying a \"totality of the circumstances\" analysis and concluding that Uber had not\nprovided sufficient disclosure of the terms); Kauders v. Uber Techs., Inc., 159 N.E.3d 1033 (Mass.\n2021); Archer v. Grubhub, Inc., 190 N.E.3d 1024 (Mass. 2022). In addition, circuit courts have\napplied state law on clickwrap enforcement in a consistent manner. E.g., Hancock v. Am. Tel. & \nTel. Co., 701 F.3d 1248 (10th Cir. 2012) (enforcing a clickwrap agreement under Oklahoma law);\nOberstein v. Live Nation Ent., Inc., 60 F.4th 505 (9th Cir. 2023) (noting close similarities between\nCalifornia and New York laws regarding online contract enforcement); Dohrmann v. Intuit, Inc.\n823 Fed. App'x 482 (9th Cir. 2020); Lee v. TicketMaster L.L.C., 817 Fed. App'x 393 (9th Cir.\n2020); Forrest v. Verizon Commc'ns, Inc., 805 A.2d 1007 (D.C. 2002).\nA similar outcome is obtained from a comprehensive empirical study of cases that are\navailable through legal search engines and secondary sources through 2022 (starting with Caspi in\n1999), from state and federal courts, addressing, explicitly or implicitly, the enforceability of\nclickwraps in consumer transactions. Out of over 100 cases, courts have enforced clickwraps, sign-\nin-wraps, and scrollwraps in every case, absent fraud or unconscionability, as long as the notice\nrequirements outlined in this Section were met. Clickwraps have been enforced in 30 states and\n62\n@§2\n"